## A Sticky Situation: Honey Gets Metallic

This week, Dakota Honey Company is in the hot seat with a series of recalls concerning their spreadable spun honey products. Stainless steel dust, flakes, and plastic shreds were found in multiple variants including Salted Caramel, Jalapeno, and Cinnamon-Infused flavors. While a subtle metallic note might add authenticity to some dishes, this is taking it a bit far.

## Marmalade Mayhem

FINESALER LLC's Alain Milliatt Marmalade Orange jars are under recall for containing glass. Yes, you read that right--glass. Something to ponder next time you're spreading sweetness on your toast.

## The Candy Conundrum

Raja Foods and Vegetables Inc. faced recalls for their Swad Masala Candies due to undeclared artificial colors like FD&C Blue 1 and Yellow 6. While vibrant-colored candy isn't inherently alarming, undeclared additives are a serious concern for food safety and consumer trust.

## The Usual Suspects

Undeclared allergens strike again, with companies like WE R NUTS recalling bridge mix due to undeclared cashews, milk, and soy. Meanwhile, Ferris Coffee and Nut Company had to pull Vanilla Bourbon Trail Mix off shelves for undisclosed wheat and soy content.

## Salmonella Makes a Comeback

Sugar Foods LLC recalled Kroger Homestyle Cheese and Garlic Croutons due to potential Salmonella contamination in the non-fat milk powder used in production. This Class I recall is a troubling reminder of the persistent threat of Salmonella in processed food ingredients.

## Prospector Popcorn Stumbles

Even Prospector Popcorn, LLC couldn't escape the allergen drama, with their Belgian Chocolate Toffee popcorn containing undeclared soy lecithin. This oversight might upset soy-allergic sweet tooths.

## Worst Performers

Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC continue their streak as top offenders. With all 11 of their recalls being Class I this year, it's evident that urgent corrective actions are necessary.

## Takeaway

While the food industry faces challenges, recurring offenders and continued lapses in quality control suggest systemic issues. It's high time for stronger scrutiny and proactive measures. Consumers should stay informed and vigilant; check your pantry and keep an eye on the recall lists.
